name: Gradle Package

on:
  release:
    types: [created]

jobs:
  build:

    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      packages: write

    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    - name: Set up JDK 17
      uses: actions/setup-java@v4
      with:
        java-version: '17'
        distribution: 'temurin'
        server-id: github # Value of the distributionManagement/repository/id field of the pom.xml
        settings-path: ${{ github.workspace }} # location for the settings.xml file

    - name: Setup Gradle
      uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@af1da67850ed9a4cedd57bfd976089dd991e2582 # v4.0.0

    - name: Build with Gradle
      run: ./gradlew build

    # The USERNAME and TOKEN need to correspond to the credentials environment variables used in
    # the publishing section of your build.gradle
    - name: Publish to GitHub Packages
      run: ./gradlew publish
      env:
        USERNAME: ${{ github.actor }}
        TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
Gradle Publish Workflow Analysis

This is GitHub's default template for publishing a Gradle package to GitHub Packages when a release is created. Here's a breakdown and some potential issues to watch for.

What it does

1. Trigger: Runs when a GitHub release is created
2. Environment: Ubuntu latest, JDK 17 (Temurin), with contents: read and packages: write permissions
3. Steps:
   · Checks out the repo
   · Sets up JDK 17 with GitHub Packages as the server
   · Installs Gradle
   · Runs ./gradlew build
   · Runs ./gradlew publish using USERNAME/TOKEN env vars

Things to check / likely issues

1. USERNAME and TOKEN env var names must match build.gradle

The workflow exposes credentials as USERNAME and TOKEN. Your build.gradle(.kts) publishing block must reference exactly those names, e.g.:

```groovy
publishing {
    repositories {
        maven {
            name = "GitHubPackages"
            url = uri("https://maven.pkg.github.com/OWNER/REPO")
            credentials {
                username = System.getenv("USERNAME")
                password = System.getenv("TOKEN")
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Note: USERNAME is risky — on Linux runners USER is already set, and some Gradle plugins read USERNAME too. Consider renaming to GITHUB_ACTOR / GITHUB_TOKEN directly to avoid confusion.

2. Missing settings.xml-based credentials

You set server-id: github and settings-path, but that only helps Maven publishing, not Gradle. For Gradle you're relying on env vars — fine, just make sure they're wired up as above.

3. Release type — usually you want published, not created

```yaml
on:
  release:
    types: [created]
```

created fires even for draft releases (which aren't public and often shouldn't be published). Most projects use:

```yaml
on:
  release:
    types: [published]
```

4. ./gradlew publish publishes all repositories

If your build also declares a non-GitHub repo (e.g. Maven Central), publish will try them all and fail. Safer:

```yaml
- name: Publish to GitHub Packages
  run: ./gradlew publishAllPublicationsToGitHubPackagesRepository
```

(or whatever your repo is named).

5. GITHUB_TOKEN scope

The default token can push to the same repo's packages only. If you publish to another repo's namespace (e.g. a shared org namespace), you need a PAT stored as a secret.

6. Version sanity

GITHUB_REF on release events is a tag like v1.2.3. If your build derives version from git tags, make sure the v prefix is stripped — many Gradle release plugins expect the version without v.

7. Permissions: packages: write alone may be insufficient

Add id-token: write if you later switch to Trusted Publishing, and contents: read is fine as-is.

Suggested cleaner version

```yaml
name: Gradle Package

on:
  release:
    types: [published]

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      packages: write

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up JDK 17
        uses: actions/setup-java@v4
        with:
          java-version: '17'
          distribution: 'temurin'

      - name: Setup Gradle
        uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@v4

      - name: Build
        run: ./gradlew build

      - name: Publish to GitHub Packages
        run: ./gradlew publishAllPublicationsToGitHubPackagesRepository
        env:
          GITHUB_ACTOR: ${{ github.actor }}
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
```

Then in build.gradle use System.getenv("GITHUB_ACTOR") / System.getenv("GITHUB_TOKEN").

